<Rive version="1" kind="fragment">
    <!--
      Source markup for assets/state_return_reuse.riv. README.md, next to
      this file, says how to rebuild it.

      What it is for: a state machine whose layers go back to states they
      just left, with transitions that mix, so a test can run it with and
      without reusing the state left (see state_change_reuse_test.cpp).
      - Side: Left (Box drifting right, red, looping) and Right (Box moving
        and lowered, blue, ping pong), switched by right, mixing over 300 ms.
        Left to Right can be cut short (enableEarlyExit); Right to Left
        can't.
      - Cycle: A, B and C (Dot at three places and colors, one shot), led by
        step: A to B at 1, B to C at 2, C to A and B to A at 0, mixing over
        200 ms. A to B can be cut short.
      - Bound: P (Mark at target, a keyframe value bound through Ease
        Target, which eases it over 500 ms) and Q (Mark parked), switched
        instantly by flip. Q also sets Mark's y from level, bound once. A
        machine going back to P while Mark is still easing has to start the
        ease over, and back to Q take level as it is then, as new instances
        would.
      - Input: U (Pin at target, bound) and V (Pin parked), switched by the
        go input, which still works once the view model is unbound. Back in
        U after an unbind, Pin has no bound value, as in a new instance.
    -->
    <Artboard defaultStateMachineId="0:7" viewModelId="0:40" width="300" height="300" name="Main" id="0:2">
        <Shape x="50" y="50" name="Box" id="0:10">
